{"id":"colt-single-action-army","slug":"colt-single-action-army","name":"Colt Single Action Army","aliases":["Colt SSA","Colt SAA","USFA Rodeo","Colt Bisley","USFA Rodeo II","Colt Peacemaker","USFA Gunslinger","USFA US Pre-War","Colt Model 1873","Colt Peacekeeper","Single Action Army","Colt .45 Peacemaker","Frontier six shooter","Frontier Six-Shooter","Colt Frontier Six-Shooter","USFA Custer Battlefield Gun","USFA Henry Nettleton Cavalry","Colt Single Action Army handgun","Colt single-action army revolver","Colt Single Action Army Revolver"],"kind":"firearm","category":"revolvers","subcategory":".45 caliber U.S. service revolver","description":"The Colt Single Action Army is a single-action revolver. It was designed for the U.S. government service revolver trials of 1872 by Colt's Patent Firearms Manufacturing Company and was adopted as the standard-issued revolver of the U.S. Army from 1873 to 1892.","history":"Bound by the Rollin White patent (#12,648, April 3, 1855), and not wanting to pay a royalty fee to Smith & Wesson, Colt could not begin development of bored-through revolver cylinders for metallic cartridge use until April 4, 1869. For the design, Colt turned to two of its best engineers: William Mason and Charles Brinckerhoff Richards who had developed a number of revolvers and black powder conversions for the company. Their effort was designed for the United States government service revolver trials of 1872 by Colt's Patent Firearms Manufacturing Company and adopted as the standard military service revolver. Production began in 1873 with the Single Action Army model 1873, also referred to as the \"New Model Army Metallic Cartridge Revolving Pistol\".\nThe first production Single Action Army, serial number 1, thought lost for many years after its production, was found in a barn in Nashua, New Hampshire, in the early 1900s. This gun was chambered in .44 S&W, a centerfire design containing charges of up to 40 grains (2.6 g) of fine-grained black powder and a 255-grain (16.5 g) blunt roundnosed bullet. Relative to period cartridges and most later handgun rounds, it was quite powerful in its full loading.\nThe Colt Single Action Army revolver, along with the 1870 and 1875 Smith & Wesson American and Model 3 \"Schofield\" revolvers, replaced the Colt 1860 Army Percussion revolver.  The Colt quickly gained favor over the S&W and remained the primary U.S. military sidearm until 1892 when it was replaced by the .38 Long Colt caliber Colt Model 1892, a double-action revolver with swing-out cylinder. By the end of 1874, serial no. 16,000 was reached; 12,500 Colt Single Action Army revolvers chambered for the .45 Colt cartridge had entered service and the remaining revolvers were sold in the civilian market.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Cartridge & Action","items":[{"label":"Cartridge","value":".45 Colt"},{"label":"Cartridge","value":".44-40 WCF"},{"label":"Cartridge","value":".38-40 WCF"},{"label":"Cartridge","value":".32-20 WCF"},{"label":"Action","value":"Single-action revolver"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"6-round cylinder"}]},{"group":"Dimensions","items":[{"label":"Weight","value":"2.31 lbs (1.048 kg; with 7 in. barrel)","numeric":2.31,"unit":"lbs"},{"label":"Length","value":"10.25 in. (260 mm, with 4 in. barrel)","numeric":10.25,"unit":"in"},{"label":"Length","value":"11 in. (279 mm, with 5 in. barrel)","numeric":11,"unit":"in"},{"label":"Length","value":"13 in. (330 mm, with 7 in. barrel)","numeric":13,"unit":"in"}]},{"group":"Production","items":[{"label":"Designer","value":"William Mason and Charles Brinckerhoff Richards"},{"label":"Designed","value":"1872"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"Colt's Patent Firearms Manufacturing Company"},{"label":"Produced","value":"1873–1941"},{"label":"Produced","value":"1956–1974"},{"label":"Produced","value":"1976–1982"},{"label":"Produced","value":"1994–present"},{"label":"Number Built","value":"457,000+"}]},{"group":"Service","items":[{"label":"Origin","value":"Unit